            "id": "wall",
            "type": "text",
            "x": 55,
            "y": 200,
            "text": "Great question! DNS (the Domain Name System) is a hierarchical,\ndistributed naming system that translates human-readable domain\nnames into IP addresses. When you type a URL, your browser first\nchecks its own cache, then the operating system's resolver cache,\nand then queries a recursive resolver, typically operated by your\nISP or a public provider. If the resolver has no cached answer, it\nstarts an iterative lookup at one of the 13 root server clusters,\nwhich respond with a referral to the relevant top-level domain (TLD)\nservers, such as those for .com. The TLD servers then refer the\nresolver to the authoritative name servers for the domain, which\nreturn the final A or AAAA record, along with a TTL that controls\nhow long the answer may be cached. Along the way, records such as\nCNAME, NS, MX and TXT may be involved, and DNSSEC can add signatures\nthat let resolvers verify each response. Modern setups may also use\nDNS over HTTPS or DNS over TLS to encrypt queries, anycast routing\nto reduce latency, and negative caching for names that do not exist.\nIt is also worth noting that resolvers may apply prefetching,\nserve-stale behaviour and query name minimisation, while operators\nbalance TTL values against propagation delays whenever records\nchange. In summary, DNS is a layered system of caches and referrals\nthat, together, map names to addresses quickly and reliably, while\nbalancing performance, security, and decentralization across the\nglobal internet...",
            "fontSize": 17,
            "fontFamily": "normal",
            "strokeColor": "#495057"
          }
        },
